  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 348 AM CDT Sun Oct 4 2026\n\nThrough tonight: greatest rain chances through the week are expected \ntoday and local radars already indicate the rain will get an early \nstart. Early morning satellite imagery/RAP analysis showed the \ncirculation around a southern stream upper level trough from the mid \nMississippi valley back across the southern Plains that will swing \nacross our region through tonight. Our airmass was rather moist with \nPWATs running around two inches per the 00Z Sun JAN sounding. As the \ntrough approaches this morning, and daytime heating commences, \nshowers and a few thunderstorms are expected to develop and move \nacross the CWA. There was still a weak stationary frontal boundary\nnearly along a line from West Point to Bastrop. The main focus \nfor convection today is expected to be along and south of this \nboundary that will remain in place today before shifting southeast\ntonight as the upper level trough shifts east of Mississippi. The\nearly start to convection should limit overall instability today \nbut there may be some locally heavy downpours in areas where the \nstorms train. /22/ \n\nThrough next weekend...\n\nGuidance has shifted a bit tonight compared to 24 hours ago and \ncoverage of stratiform showers didn't quite pan out as expected. It \nstill looks like the main synoptic front will make headway into our \nnorthern areas, bringing a push of dry air Monday into Tuesday, \nhowever it stalls as it loses upper support near the I-20 corridor \nand struggles to push further south. This means that for areas south \nof I-20, the airmass will remain largely unchanged. Low rain chances \n(20% or less) could persist into Tuesday south of I-20, a little \nlater than previous forecasts. High pressure will establish \neasterlies over the area which will push a backdoor front into the \narea. This now looks to be the main push of drier air mid week \nbefore a a shortwave/upper low out of Texas promote return flow. As \nstated previously, lapse rates are moist adiabatic and don't \nsupport severe, but the deep moisture could potentially lead to a \nheavy rain threat that necessitates monitoring. GFS is most \naggressive with this with a prominent Gulf low feature, other \nmodels are weaker, which is probably the more likely scenario. The\narea of heaviest rain potential is teetering on the edge of the \nCWA. It very well could be east of our area, or is it could be in \nthe I-59 corridor, but it is too uncertain to tell at this time. \n/SAS/",
